## Introduction
In the last few decades, organic framework materials have attracted massive attention in chemistry and materials science due to the possibility of designing a large variety of aesthetically interesting structures. The unique features of these materials, including modifiable surface area, porosity, and multifunctional flexibility, are favorable for various applications including gas storage and separation, as well as catalysis. Apart from these conventional applications, the excessive porosity, broad circumferential topology as well as great solvent and thermal stability make them suitable for organic skeleton in batteries [1, 2], stationary phase for columns in chromatographic separation [3, 4], sensing [5-12] and biomedicine [13, 14].  Moreover, it has been envisioned that the basic concept of controlling the structure, surface area, and porosity may ultimately result in unprecedented applications and commercialization of these materials in various fields.
MOF has been the most studied organic framework, comprising of metal ions and organic ligands assembled in designed coordination. The first MOF was reported back in 1995 by Yaghi and coworkers based on Co atoms and 1,3,5-benzentricarboxylate (BTC) linkers, which has brought a milestone in the history of organic framework materials research [15]. On the other hand, COF is another group of organic framework materials discovered in 2005, which generally consists of solely organic linker blocks [16]. Furthermore, the emerging graphene and its derivatives (e.g., graphene oxide (GO) and reduced graphene oxides (rGO)) not only initialized the exciting two-dimensional (2D) materials field but revolutionized the fabrication of organic framework materials as well. The so-called “GOF” is composed of individual graphene/derivatives 2D layers connected with organic linkers. The first GOF was realized in 2010 by Burress et al through the use of benzene-1,4-diboronic acid (B14DBA) pillars as the interconnection between 2D layers [17].
As the organic linker is the key component among the aforementioned three types of organic framework materials, specific attention has been paid to linker design to effectively modulate surface areas and porosity [18-21]. Diversified chemical functionalities, on the other hand, can be induced in the organic framework through the proper engineering of the linkers [20-25]. Apart from engineering the linker parameter, however, some desired properties and functionalities of the organic frameworks cannot be realized via direct synthesis. Therefore, post-synthesis modifications become critical in most cases of framework engineering. Moreover, the wet chemistry synthesis approach is currently the gold standard for organic framework fabrication [26-30]. The slight variation in reaction conditions may show a significant influence on tuning the crystallinity, morphology, and production yield [31-37]. Although there have been a few review articles discussing particular engineering techniques of MOFs, COFs and GOFs separately [38-43], it is rare to have a critical and comprehensive review on the engineering of all these types of organic framework materials from the basic element selection, synthesis conditions, eventually to the post-synthesis modification. Here we present a comprehensive review focusing on the recent advances in tuning the structures of the MOFs, GOFs, and COFs and their subsequent effects on properties and applications. At first, each type of organic framework materials is introduced highlighting the characteristics and classification. Later, the tuning strategies of structures of organic framework materials with respect to the type and length of ligands, various reaction conditions (e.g., temperature, time, and reactant), and post-synthesis modifications are summarized and critically discussed. Afterward, the influences of structural tuning on the electronic, mechanical, chemical, and thermal properties are reviewed. The impacts of performances of different applications such as catalysis, desalination, gas sensing, and gas storage are presented. Finally, significant remarks and prospects in this field are provided.
## 2. Types of organic frameworks
2.1  Metal-Organic Framework:
MOFs are generated through the coordination bonds of metal cations/chunk with the organic ligands [34, 44]. The metal-containing units or secondary building units (SBUs), are associated with the ligands and thus leads to the development of a porous framework (Figure 1a) [25]. An exceptionally large set of noteworthy MOF structures can be obtained through varying metal ions, linkers, and postsynthetic modifications, leading to diverse chemical behaviors by rationally designing functional MOFs. The molecular-scale porosity, surface area, and density for MOFs have been achieved up to $94\%$ free volume, $7800~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and $0.13\ \mathrm{gcm}^{-3}$ , respectively [45-49]. Such impressive features have made MOFs suitable candidates for various applications including drug delivery [50, 51], sensor [52- 54], proton conductors [55, 56], and gas storage [57].
MOFs obtain internal microporous structures which can accommodate guest species such as molecules, clusters, or particles to serve as catalytic sites. MOFs can be classified in diverse groups according to stages of synthesis, crystal structure management, stimuli, and structural frameworks [58- 61]. Conventionally, Kitagawa and Kondo classified MOFs into three generations (shown in Figure 1d-f) [62]: (1) The first-generation compounds possess unstable porous structures which collapse after the elimination of the guest. (2) The second-generation compounds hold framework rigidity upon guest withdrawal and generally exhibit type I adsorption isotherms. (3) The third-generation compounds are considered as “soft” since the frameworks are malleable and distinguished by “stepped” or “S-shaped” isotherms [63-68]. For a simpler statement, the $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ generation MOFs are considered as the standard MOFs consisting with organic linkers and metal ion centers, while $2^{\mathrm{nd}}$ generation MOFs are based on the $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ generation with surface chemical modifications to yield robust porous structures [69]. UiO-66 MOF is an example of the $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ generation $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF whereas the UiO-66 MOFs with functional groups are examples of $2^{\mathrm{nd}}$ generation Zr-MOF. On the other hand, the flexible MOFs or soft porous crystals such as PCN-700 is the example of $3^{\mathrm{rd}}$ generation $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF featured with “soft” or “flexible” [70, 71]. Typically, flexible MOFs are capable of reversible changes in the structural conformations responding to external incentives e.g., pH, ions, magnetic field, light, temperature, and pressure. Alternatively, in the case of rigid MOFs, their conformations do not change in the presence of such external factors [72].
Moreover, Liu et al. [73-75] introduced the concept of “fourth-generation MOFs” which have “modifiable” pore size and chemical properties via post-synthetic approaches (Figure 1g, part i) [74- 76]. On the other hand, Kitagawa also defined the fourth-generation MOFs as complex systems containing anisotropy and defects through post-synthesis modification (Figure 1g, parts ii and iii) [68]. On another note, the features of the $4^{\mathrm{th}}$ generation MOFs are “hybrid and hierarchy”, “anisotropy and asymmetry”, and “defect and disorder” or simply “HAD” in short [77]. These are particularly MOFs with asymmetry or anisotropy in their networks, MOF crystals with size-dependent properties, and hybrid systems such as $\mathrm{MOF}_{1}$ -on- $\mathrm{.MOF}_{2}$ and MOF on $\mathrm{\Delta}X$ (other materials). For example, ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}(2,$ $2^{\prime}$ ‐bipyridine‐5, $5^{\prime}$ ‐dicarboxylate or bpydc) $_{2}\mathrm{MOF}$ hybridized with $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ (biphenyl‐4, $4^{\prime}$ ‐dicarboxylate or bpdc)2 MOF on the ${\mathrm{Cu(OH)}}_{2}$ substrate is an example of $4^{\mathrm{th}}$ generation MOF [78]. However, the fourth generation MOFs have been considered as stiff frameworks with self-switching pores which are adaptable to a particular guest through a reshuffle of extra framework counterions or amendment of flexible ligand substituents (Figure 1g, part iv, and part v).
Furthermore, in accordance with the structural dimension, MOFs can be divided into four categories, which are zero-dimension (0D), one-dimension (1D), 2D, and three-dimension (3D). Very recently, Duan et al. introduced $\mathrm{~a~}0\mathrm{D}$ nickel and iron-based bimetallic MOF with remarkable electronic and structural characteristics for electrocatalysis [79]. Besides, an array of hollow 1D MOF superstructure was reported for uranium entrapment [80]. The ultrathin 2D MOF nanosheets with excessive intrinsic porosities, plentiful reachable active sites as well as high surface areas have gained enormous attention in numerous functional electronic devices. On the other hand, MOF-5 is a popular example of 3D MOF for hydrocarbon adsorption and separation [81].
The specific characteristic of MOFs depends on various factors and thus can be tuned accordingly. For instance, the electronic conductivity of MOFs depends on the concentrations of free charge carriers. Therefore, both the metal cations and the organic linkers may have a significant impact in this regard.
On the other hand, the mechanical strength of these materials relies greatly on the type and length of ligands. Although both the chemical and thermal properties are associated to the strength of the metallinker bond, thermal properties also depend on the number of ligands attached to each node in the MOF structure since thermal degradation occurs by the breakage of the bond between node and ligand, together with or afterward the combustion of the ligand.
2.2 Graphene-Organic Framework:
GOFs are another type of porous framework in which organic linkers are used to interconnect 2D GO sheets and their derivatives (Figure 1b) [17]. GOFs have gained significant attention, particularly for energy-associated approaches due to their multifunctional flexibility [17, 82-90]. Being a layered material, GO consists of graphene sheets that contain hydrophilic oxygens along with different oxygen groups on their edges and basal planes [91, 92]. The interlayer spacing is modifiable either through intercalation or covalent bonding with several chemical species/groups which leads to the tuning of porosity as well as accessible surface area [93-96]. Therefore, the catalytic activity and efficiency for electrochemical or gas storage-related applications can be improved. In addition, the expansion of the interlayer spacing results in materials with higher surface areas and plenty of beneficial active sites for catalytic applications [97, 98].
The properties of GOFs mostly depend on the type of linkers. Moreover, the electronic properties of GOFs depend on the linker concentration. On the other hand, the chemical properties of these substances depend on the synthesis temperature and the thermal stability shows a variation with the toughness of the formed bond between the linker and GO layers.
