Covalent-Organic Framework:
COFs are a group of porous framework polymeric materials composed of organic building units formed through strong covalent bonds. Different from MOFs, an important aspect of COFs is that these are structurally pre-designable, functionally manageable, and synthetically controllable. Moreover, they have low mass densities, tunable pore size and structure, high thermal stability, a large surface area, facilely tailored functionality, and permanent porosity. All these features have made the COFs a potential candidate for extended applications in adsorption, catalysis, and gas storage.
Similar to MOFs, COFs can be either 1D, 2D, and 3D varied by the building block dimension. Very recently, Xu et al. introduced single-crystalline 1D metallo-COFs by associating the dynamic covalent chemistry (DCC) and metal-ligand coordination [103]. The first 2D crystalline porous organic frameworks, COF-1 and COF-5 (Figure 1c, part i) and 3D COF materials (Figure 1c, part ii) were introduced by Yaghi and his co-workers in 2005 and 2007 respectively [16, 104]. Hence, a variety of different topologies can be employed to design the 2D COFs. For the orientation of COFs, both regular (isotropic topologies) and irregular (anisotropic topologies) types of polygon-shaped frameworks have been evolved. Each of the lattice structures is thoroughly organized with discrete pores. Here, the structural diversification of 2D COFs comes from the topological distinctiveness. Moreover, there are plenty of COFs having various building blocks behind each topology. The 2D COFs are suitable to emerge new p-type photo functional and electronic materials for applications in photovoltaics and optoelectronics.
On the other hand, the design of 3D COFs requires at least one building unit whereas 2D COFs generally demand planar building blocks. Besides, in comparison with 2D counterparts, 3D COFs are seldom pre-designable and synthetically manageable owing to the interpenetrated structure. They possess high surface area and hence, low densities which have made these materials a potential candidate for gas storage applications.
Moreover, COFs can be primarily divided into three types focusing on the variety of the building blocks, including boron-containing, imine-based, and triazine-based COFs [105]. Imine-based frameworks can be further divided into two different types subjected to the covalent generation modes of $-C=\Nu-$ bonds [106], which are hydrazone-linked (developed through the co-condensation mechanism of hydrazides with aldehydes [107]) and ‘‘Schiff base’’ (generated through the cocondensation of amines with aldehydes [108]). Furthermore, triazine-based COFs, or covalent triazinebased frameworks (CTFs), emerged by cyclotrimerization of nitrile ligands [109]. Finally, boroncontaining COFs are generally synthesized through co-condensation of two or more organic blocks, or by self-condensation of a single unit.
The electronic properties of COFs can be engineered through the appropriate modifications of the chain length. On the contrary, the mechanical stability varies with COF topology since COFs containing central carbon atoms have been found steadier compared to those having other atoms e.g., silicon. Moreover, the nature of the covalent bonding controls the chemical properties of the materials.
Regarding thermal conductivity, porosity has been found as the main structural variable of COFs since the bigger the pores, the lesser heat should be transported across the material.
3. Tuning on the structural design of organic framework materials:
3.1 Tuning of organic framework materials with a variety of ligands:
3.1.1 MOFs with different types of ligands:
(a) Carboxylate ligands:
Multicarboxylate ligands are widely used for structuring organic framework materials since these ligands have a large variety of coordination modes [110-113]. Some popular examples of such materials include MOF-5, HKUST-1, UiO-66, MIL-88 containing zinc, copper, zirconium, and iron metal ions, respectively.
i. Co-based MOFs:
Co-MOF-71 with benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid (BDC) linker was initiated by Yaghi et al for the first time in 2005 [114]. The framework was composed of unlimited chains of corner-sharing ${\mathrm{CoO}}_{6}$ octahedra, while each of the chains was connected to four parallelly moving chains through the BDC ligands. Later, Miles et al. illustrated that a “conformal transformation” (i.e. the crystal shape persists, Figure 2a, part i) occurred for Co-MOF-71 exposed to alkaline aqueous media and resulted in the formation of porous and highly dispersed cobalt hydroxide showing great pseudo capacitance and electrocatalytic performance [115].
On the other hand, Yang et al. synthesized a layered Co-MOF material with the same (BDC) ligand demonstrating a nanosheet morphology (Figure 2a, part ii). The crystal structure consisted of two parallel edges sharing ${\mathrm{CoO}}_{6}$ octahedral chains which were connected with $\upmu^{3}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{OH}$ groups.  The chains were bent and each of the carboxylate groups formed three bonds to the cobalt atoms and the terephthalate groups bridged the layers to form the 3D structure. The conductive layered structure and thin nanosheets of this material led to good electrochemical properties e.g., high specific capacitance (max. $2564\mathrm{Fg^{-1}}$ ), wonderful cycling stability, and superior rate capability [116].
ii. Zr-based MOFs:
Cavka et al. used BDC ligand to form a high surface area and significantly stable Zirconium $(Z\mathbf{r})$ MOF, named “UiO- ${\cdot66}^{\prime\prime}$ [117]. Since the strong interaction between group IV elements and oxygen, oxygencontaining ligands are favorable to assist in the production of a stable inorganic foundation. The
structure of UiO-66 with the complete chemical formula of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ has 12 coordination numbers of metal ions in close-packed cubic metal structures.
iii. Fe-based MOFs:
Zhang et al. synthesized iron (III) carboxylate MOFs with various linkers [118]. Among them, MIL 88 materials exhibited a flexible structure with an ability to tune the pore size in presence of external factors (temperature, pressure, or guest adsorption). The structure and textural properties of these materials have been summarized in Table-1.
iv. Zn-based MOFs:
Chandrasekhar et al. introduced a homochiral Zn-MOF with pyrene-tetralactic acid (PLA) ligand, ZnPLA [119]. The assembly of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions and the organic linker PLA throve to the synthesis of a 3D porous anionic framework structure (Figure 2b, part i).
Sarkar et al. synthesized a Zn-BTC MOF, $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{n}}$ by heating BTC and $\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}$ in DMF (N,N- dimethyl formamide) [120]. The 3D structure was formed by dinuclear zinc carboxylate paddlewheel sections while water molecules inhabited on axial points (Figure 2b, part ii). Having pentagonal geometry, each $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ atom was coordinated with four carboxylate groups of four $\mathrm{BTC}^{3-}$ units, and the fifth position was occupied by one water molecule. The structure and textural properties of several Zn-based MOFs have been summarized in table-1.
v. Cu-based MOFs:
Shete et al. demonstrated the generation of $\mathrm{Cu(BDC)}$ nanosheets through straight uniform blending of the metal and BDC linker solutions addressing the bottom-up synthesis approach [121]. Later, the DMF-soaked sheets upon exchanging solvent with $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ brought desolvation and were identified as having disorderedness in orientation. On the other hand, Hosseini et al. reported the synthesis of the uniform, adhesive and nanoporous stable Cu-BTC MOF films by an electrochemical approach [122]. The structure and textural properties of Cu-based MOFs with carboxylate ligands have been summarized in Table-1.
(b) Phosphonate ligands:
The coordination of phosphonate linkers with different metal ions varies on several factors e.g., the nature of the metal ions, the type of phosphonates, molar ratio, substituents on the linker filament, and reaction conditions. Being a strong anionic moiety, the phosphanate group influences to emerge strong bonds with metal ions. The phosphonates are more compatible with the metal ions compared to the carboxylate ligands, however, the coordination chemistry of phosphonates is less anticipated because of having several coordination modes. Examples of MOFs with phosphonate ligands include those having cobalt, copper, and vanadium metal ions, etc.
LeBideau and co-workers introduced various copper(II) phosphonates: $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{RPO}_{3}).\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} $ , $\mathfrak{a}$ -Cu (RPO3) $[\mathrm{R}{=}{\bf-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{H}_{3},{\bf-}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5},{\bf-}\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}]$ , and $\mathrm{Cu_{3}O(C H_{3}P O_{3})_{2}}–2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , ${\upbeta}{\mathrm{-Cu}}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{3})$ . The ${\upbeta}{\mathrm{-Cu}}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{3})$ crystallized as rhombohedral whereas $\mathrm{Cu_{3}O(C H_{3}P O_{3})_{2}.2H_{2}O}$ was found as in monoclinic (Figure 2c, part i) crystal structure [123]. Peeples et al reported a conductive phosphonate MOF, $\begin{array}{r}{\left[\{\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\}\right.}\end{array}$ (2,6-NDPA)0.5] 1 $\mathrm{\Delta(NDPA=}$ naphthalenediphosphonic acid), consisting of a 2D inorganic building unit (IBU) [124]. The 2D IBUs, having a continuous edge-sharing sheet of copper phosphonate polyhedral, formed the 3D pillared-layered MOF with a connection through polyaromatic 2,6-NDPA ligands. The textural properties of this MOF have been summarized in Table-1.
