(c) Nitrogen-containing ligands:
Nitrogen-containing ligands, e.g., tris (4(1H-imidazol-1-yl) phenyl) amine (TIPA), tri(4- pyridylphenyl) amine (TPPA) [125-129] are another popular group for realizing MOFs. The presence of N donor atoms in these ligands leads to great coordination possibility with transition metal cations. Moreover, these organic compounds can take up distinct coordination geometries in association with the various characteristics of metal ions.
3.1.2 GOFs with different types of ligands:
Boronic acids are very much likely to yield boronic esters [130]. The covalent bond between boron and oxygen is stronger than typical C-O bonds although the condensation reaction is fast as well as reversible [131]. Thus, boronic acids have been employed as ligands for the synthesis of GOF structures due to the boronate ester formation through the oxygen functional groups of the GO layers. In such an investigation, porous GOFs were prepared with linear boronic acids by pillaring the layered GO sheets [18].
Moreover, comparative study with other commonly used ligands for the generation of MOFs and COFs such as BDC, BTC, benzene-1,2,3,4,5,6- hexacarboxylic acid, 4,5-dicyanoimidazole, 2- methylimidazole, and imidazole were also tried under the same solvothermal condition to react with the GO [17]. However, none of these organic blocks could significantly intercalate like conventional GOFs and failed to possess any hydrogen adsorption might be because of different chemistry.
