3.1.3 COFs with different types of building blocks:
The earliest COF was prepared through the self-condensation of the boronic acid ligands to bring boroxine anhydride-based connections patterning ${\bf B}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ rings. For example, the crystalline COF-1 was synthesized through the self-condensation of B14DBA to form an arrangement with an expansion of layers piled in the staggered form and yielded hexagonal pores having a diameter of $15\mathrm{\AA}$ (Figure 3a, part i) [16]. Moreover, boronic acids can also produce boronate esters from reaction with catechols, some examples of this type of COFs are COF-105  and COF-5 (Figure 3a, part ii and iii) [16, 104]. Another example is COF-202 where boronic acids formed borosilicate bonds from reaction with silanols [132].
Recently, the formation of B-N bond has been implemented to synthesize COFs, in which the borazine ring connections are formed through the fragmentation of the amine borane-functionalized ligands [133]. In addition, COFs have been prepared using the nitrogen-containing linkers via the generation of C-N bonds. COF-300, a material with a 5-fold interpenetrated dia topology, is the first example of this type of COFs, which is connected through the imine condensation of the aldehyde and amine ligands (Figure 3b, part i) [134-136]. Similar to the condensation of boronic acids, imine bond formation has been proven as a flexible technique for the production of COFs and plenty of materials have been synthesized through the identical reaction [137, 138]. In parallel, the formation of $\mathrm{C-N}$ bond has been utilized to generate COFs containing hydrazone connections, e.g. COF-42 and COF-43 [139]. These COFs are yielded via the condensation of aldehydes and hydrazide linkers.
Covalent triazine-based frameworks or CTFs, comprise a distinct type of COFs, synthesized at ionothermal state through the cyclical trimerization of the cyano reaction groups (-CN) [140, 141]. Due to the low reversibility of the trimerization reaction, these materials hold poor crystallinity. The structures of CTF-1 and CTF-2 have been shown in the following figure (figure 3b, part i and ii). In addition, polyamide COFs have been generated more recently through the successful demonstration of the imidization reaction [142].
