From the discussion above, the organic linkers have a direct influence on the structure of the organic framework materials. Especially, the type of chemical bond and the coordination geometry of the synthesized materials both largely depend on the used ligand. The aromatic carboxylic acids are widely used as ligands for the synthesis of MOFs due to the availability, diversity of coordination modes, facile synthesis methods, and formation of superior quality crystals with most of the metal ions. Besides, based on the degree of deprotonation and crystal packing arrangement, the aromatic polycarboxylate ligands may act as convenient H-bond donors and acceptors, leading to facile crystallization with the formation of additionally stable MOF structures. However, the insufficient chemical stability and low thermal stability of the carboxylate group impose a serious concern for carboxylate-based MOFs for industrial or commercial applications [143-147].
The other ligands studied for the synthesis of MOFs are the phosphanate ligands and those containing nitrogen as donor atoms, both of which are capable to form stronger bonds with metal centers compared to those of carboxylate ligands. Phosphanates have three oxygen atoms, which are capable to coordinate metals in any state of protonation and therefore open the door to host many possible modes of coordination with various structural arrangements. Notwithstanding, the difficulty to obtain quality crystals and porous compounds with phosphanate linkers limits their suitability for the synthesis of MOFs. Similarly, it is challenging to achieve crystal complexes with nitrogen-containing ligands (e.g. pyrazolate ligands), which makes it troublesome to utilize these ligands targeting the formation of MOFs. On the other hand, boronic acid ligands are particularly useful for the synthesis of GOFs and COFs, which are unlikely to form coordination bonds with metal cations to yield MOFs due to the incomplete octet of boron.
However, it is not only the ligand which leads to the formation of high-quality products but appropriate reaction conditions and complementing geometry between the reactants as well. For example, in the case of the solvothermal reaction of MOFs, it is a Lewis acid-base reaction with the deprotonated ligands as the Lewis base and the metal ions as the Lewis acids. Reaction kinetics is therefore very important, in which faster reactions may lead to bulk precipitation instead of single crystal MOFs. Therefore, it is crucial to modify the reaction conditions to match the rate of ligand deprotonation with the coordination bond formation. Since there is no universal reaction condition incorporating all ligands and metal centers, it is extremely challenging to design new metal-ligand pairs as it demands plenty of active linkers for the optimization of synthesis methods.
3.2 Functional groups in the ligands:
3.2.1 Carboxylate ligands with functional groups:
Boron-rich dicarboxylate p-carborane ligands can be used for designing MOFs which are strong as well as highly porous. The carboxylate-based carborane ligands were synthesized following the $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ catalyzed coupling chemistry. The length of these ligands exceeded the other dicarboxylated pcarborane ligands and thus resulted in MOF structures with greater porosity. In addition, these MOFs possessed remarkably high surface areas compared to their single carborane precursors [148].
Hu et al. incorporated different types of functional groups into the host UiO-66 MOF, such as UiO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , UiO-66- $(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ , UiO-66- $(\mathrm{COOH})_{2}$ , UiO-66- $\mathrm{(F)}_{4}$ , UiO-66- $(\mathrm{OCH}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{3})_{2}$ , and UiO-66- $\mathrm{(COOH)_{4}}$ with 2-Aminoterephthalic acid (ATC), 2,5-Dihydroxyterephthalic acid (DOBDC), 1,2,4,5- Benzenetetracarboxylic acid (BTEC), 2,5-Diethyloxyterephthalic acid (DEOBDC), 2,3,5,6- Tetrafluoro-1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid (TFBDC), and Benzene-1,2,3,4,5,6-hexacarboxylic acid (BHC) respectively as ligands [23]. The PXRD patterns of most of these MOFs matched with the pattern of UiO-66 even upon the addition of various functional groups which indicated an isostructural framework topology.
3.2.2 Phosphonate ligands with functional groups:
In another study, a polyfunctional phosphonate, N, $\mathbf{N^{\prime}}$ - piperazinium bis methylene phosphonate had been utilized to design two types of polar 3D MOFs [150]. These two MOFs revealed similarities in both composition and crystal structure. Having 3D framework structures, both of the materials had identical connections on all sides of the inorganic part of the layout i.e., lanthanum phosphate. The critical basic component for each of the phases was a continuous series of lanthanum centers which were connected through linking phosphonate groups to other chains and finally expanding in the adirection. On the contrary, the difference between the two MOFs was found in the manner the “lanthanum phosphate” chains had been linked which relied on the guest anions.
3.2.3 Amine ligand with functional groups:
He et al. synthesized two microporous MOFs by assembling $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ or $\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}$ ions with a large tris((4- carboxyl) phenyl duryl) amine ligand [151]. Although both were non-interpenetrating 3D frameworks, interestingly the $\mathbf{MgMOF}$ was found much more stable compared to the Zn structure with high lifelong porosity and surface area of $1457\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
Briefly, the addition of functional groups in the ligands leads to structural diversity with an increase in the surface area and porosity of the organic framework materials. However, functional groups in ligands sometimes exhibit adverse effects. For example, MOFs synthesized from thiol functionalized ligands may suffer from a lack of crystallinity and stability [152]. Moreover, -OH and - $\left.-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right.$ functionalization of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs may result in increasing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake whereas functionalization with - COOH group responses opposite with a considerable reduction in surface area. In this case, the - $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and -OH functional groups provide more $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption sites into $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs and accelerate the affinity to uptake $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . On the other hand, -COOH functional group forms $\mathrm{COO^{-}}$ group after losing $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ , resulting in the acidic property and therefore leading to reduced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake [153]. Besides, functionalization with the amide group causes low affinity to non-polar/quadrupolar $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ molecules, limiting their implementation for adsorption of these gases [154]. Furthermore, the proportion of functional groups in the ligand also influences the structure and characteristics of the final products. In particular, Yang et. al. studied the effect of changing the molar ratio of hydroxyl group functionalization into ligands for the synthesis of Al-MIL-53 MOFs [155]. Although the final MOFs with 25 and $50\%$ molar ratio of hydroxyl functional group resulted in larger surface areas, substantial distortion of the framework structure is occurred simultaneously. To prevent such issues, a good knowledge over the chemical and structural properties of functional groups prior to chemical reaction is mandatory.
3.3 Based on the length of ligand:
3.3.1 Carboxylate ligand:
(a) IRMOFs:
Isoreticular MOFs or IRMOFs, invented by Yaghi and co-workers [156, 157] are a 3D-linked cubic framework structure where the oxide-centered $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}$ tetrahedral clusters associate through dicarboxylate ligands, acts as the metal centers. With the BDC ligand, the first representative of this group is IRMOF-1 or MOF-5 comprising a pore size of ${\sim}9\mathrm{\AA}$ . Extending the length of the BDC ligand yielded the other members of this group, e.g. IRMOF-10 and IRMOF-16 with an increase in the surface area, pore size, and pore volume [19]. The building units and the graphical demonstration of the unit cell of these MOFs have been shown in Figure 4a.
(b) UiO-66:
Expansion of the length of the linker might be a critical strategy to produce organic framework materials with an extended surface area. Cavka et al. demonstrated that it is possible to synthesize a stable UiO-66 MOF structure via enlargement of the length of the ligands [117]. In the beginning, the UiO-66 MOF possessed a Langmuir surface area of $1187~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which later yielded 3000 and 4170 $\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with an increase in the ligands to two and three benzene ring dicarboxylic acids respectively. Additionally, an extension of the length of the ligands might also generate extremely durable materials as it did not decrease the stability of the framework.
On the other hand, Ragon et al. studied the effect of the size and functional group of the fundamental dicarboxylate ligands above the crystallization kinetics of a group of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs representing the UiO66 topology [158]. Different types of ligands including 2,6-naphthalene-, $^{4,4^{,}}$ -biphenyl- and $^{3,3^{\circ}-}$ dichloro- $^{.4,4^{,}}$ -azobenzene-dicarboxylates, as well as a group of X-functionalized terephthalates
1 $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{X=NH}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ , Br, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{.}$ ), were evaluated (Figure 4b) and compared to that of initial UiO-66. Hence, the crystallization kinetics had been found to vary with the length of the linker.
3.3.2 Bis (triazole) ligands:
The choice of flexible linkers for the synthesis of MOFs has become established as these organic building units can act as a perfect synthon for generating functional porous coordination polymer materials. Particularly, the dynamic crystal transformation characteristic along with the flexibility and conformational liberty of these ligands permits them to hold to the coordination state of the metal ions and polyoxometalates (POM) templates [159].
Tian et al. incorporated the flexible organic building blocks such as 1,3-bis(1,2,4-triazol-l-yl)propane (btp); 1,4-bis(1,2,4-triazol-l-yl)butane (btb), and 1,6-bis(1,2,4-triazol-l-yl)hexane (btx) into the WellsDawson POM network targeting flexible and stable unique structures of POM coordination polymers [160]. Four Wells-Dawson polyoxoanion-based blended materials, $\mathrm{\Delta[Cu_{6}(b t p)_{3}(P_{2}W_{18}O_{62}]}.$ $3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{[Cu_{6}}}$ (btp)3 $(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\mathrm{O}_{62}]$ . $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ; $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(b t b)_{6}}$ $(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\mathrm{O}_{62}]$ . $6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and $\mathrm{\small{[Cu_{3}\ (b t x)_{5.5}\ (P_{2}W_{18}O_{62}]}}$ . $\ensuremath{4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ were prepared and structurally characterized by modifying the length of flexible bis(triazole) linkers and various metal ion coordination topologies. Hence, it was concluded that the structures of these materials were synergistically impacted by the length of the flexible bis(triazole) ligands and the metal ion coordination topologies.
3.3.3 Boronic acid ligands:
As mentioned earlier, Srinivas et al. reported the successful expansion of GO sheets utilizing different linear boronic acid linkers to prepare a group of porous GOFs under solvothermal conditions [18]. The dehydrated, vacant GOFs revealed a significantly increased surface area over the precursor GO sheets, varying with the type and length of the boronic acid linkers (Figure 4c). From the XRD results, a rise in the value of the d-spacing had been found with increasing the length of linear boronic acid pillaring units. In summary, the interlayer spacing and BET surface area of the porous framework are critically varied by tuning the length of the boronic acid linkers.
Figure 4: (a) Representation of the IRMOFs (IRMOF-1, IRMOF-10, and IRMOF-16): variation of pore size with the length of ligands, copied from ref. [19] with permission from ACS, copyright 2010; (b) The structure of the UiO-66 MOFs, reproduced from ref. [158] with permission from John Wiley and Sons.
3.3.4 Alkyl functionalized COFs:
Lavigne and co-workers reported a group of microporous alkyl (i.e., methyl, ethyl, and propyl) functionalized COFs which had been generated through the chemical reaction of benzene-1,3,5- triboronic acid with the dialkyl substituted derivatives of 1,2,4,5-tetrahydroxybenzene [22]. Unlike the previous examples, the surface area and pore volume of COFs in this case decreased with increasing the length of the alkyl substituent. This might be due to the filling of the pore by the increased bulk as the length of the alkyl substituent extends or owing to the creation of “pore corner cavities’’.
To sum up, it is possible to tune the chemical structure, porosity, reaction kinetics, and interlayer spacing of organic framework materials through modification of the length of ligands. Especially, an increase in the length of the ligands leads to a larger surface area. However, since the solubility of aromatic ligands in organic solvents decreases with increasing length, it may prevent the synthesis of solids at lower temperatures and cause slower reaction kinetics. Such an effect has been demonstrated by Ragon et el. for the case of UiO-66 Zr-MOFs realized by different sizes of carboxylate ligands [158]. Therefore, it is important to maintain the reaction kinetics with an appropriate choice of reaction conditions when modulating surface area with increasing lengths of ligands.
3.4 Based on Reaction Parameters:
3.4.1 Effect of reaction conditions on MOF synthesis:
Effect of temperature:
Reaction conditions play a vital role in influencing the synthesis of organic framework materials. Synthesis temperature is one of the most significant aspects susceptible to change in the rational design of MOFs [161]. With a variation of the reaction temperature, it is possible to identify the presence of thermodynamic or kinetic control of the generation of a particular phase. Oliveira et al. prepared two different 3D MOFs, compound 1 and compound 2, applying thulium(III) ion and succinate ligand using different hydrothermal temperatures (i.e. $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively) [31]. Both of the compounds possessed the same formula, however, compound 1 was made of colorless block-shaped crystals (Figure 5a, part i) and solidified in a monoclinic phase while compound 2 was composed of colorless needle-like crystals (Figure 5a, part ii) and emerged in a monoclinic phase. Hence, a succinate linker might come up with different coordination modes varying the synthesis temperature, which could produce various conformations of MOFs.
On the other hand, McKinstry et al. investigated the impact of specific reaction conditions on the formation mechanism of MOF-5 crystals [162]. The experiments were conducted at 140, 130, 120, and $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{\i}}$ , and only under $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the solid phase formation process follows a straightforward reaction pathway to generate an intermediate phase (labeled as MOF69c (desolv)) first and followed by the gradual transformation to the final MOF-5 products. In this process, the reaction strategy for the conversion between MOF- $69\mathrm{c}$ (desolv) and MOF-5 was presented as below:
$$
4Z\mathrm{n}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\quad\longleftrightarrow\quad3Z\mathrm{n}_{4}\mathrm{O}\left(\mathrm{BDC}\right)_{3}+5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}
$$
However, extremely complicated synthesis routes comprising numerous intermediate solid phases were revealed upon reduction of the reaction temperature beneath $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . It was impossible to separate and further characterize the intermediates owing to the existence of multiple transients and their metastable characteristic.
Effect of solvent:
The impact of solvents on materials synthesis has been described in several investigations previously, e.g., the polarity of the solvent, as well as the solubility of the ligand and its proteolysis features significantly influenced the formation of MOFs from both of the $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}/H_{2}B D C N H_{2}/s o}$ lvent and $\mathrm{Al}^{3+}/$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDCNH}_{2}/$ solvent systems [163, 164]. Bauer et al. synthesized the isostructural 3D frameworks of MIL-53, MIL-88B, and MIL-101 based on 2-aminoterephthalic acid and Fe(III), which were identified as $\mathrm{Fe-MIL}53\_\mathrm{NH_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{Fe-MIL88B\_NH_{2}}$ , and $\mathrm{Fe-MIL101\_NH_{2}}$ , respectively [144]. at lower temperatures, both in methanol and in acetonitrile solvents, only Fe-MIL88B_ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was found to be generated. while the reaction was carried out in DMF, structures consisting of the trimeric inorganic brick $[\mathrm{Fe}_{3}(\upmu_{3}\mathrm{-O})(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CR})^{6}]^{+}$ , i.e., Fe-MIL88B_ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and Fe-MIL101_ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , were separated whereas the reaction in water predominantly produced Fe-MIL53_NH2.
Another study depicted the influence of modifying solvent on the $\mathrm{Cr^{3+/}H_{3}B T C/}$ solvent system [165]. With only water as the solvent, pure CrMIL-100 was achieved whereas the inclusion of some methanol came up with a mixture of CrMIL-100 and CrMIL-96. Moreover, further addition of methanol led to the synthesis of pure CrMIL-96, and exclusion of water from the synthesis mixture led to the generation of the same phase, however, with low crystallinity. Here, MIL-100(Cr) represented a zeolite type structure while MIL-96(Cr) had a distinct phase emerging from the coordination of $\mathrm{Cr^{3+}}$ with $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ . According to the XRD patterns of MIL-96 and MIL-100 shown in Figure 5c, their structures are absolutely different despite using very similar reaction conditions. Thus, owing to the distinct solubilities and polarities of the reaction mixture, the tuning of solvent might result in an extremely inconsistent manner of the reaction environment even though the starting material and the other conditions didn’t vary.
Effect of reactant composition:
Choi et al. investigated the effect of ligand composition on the solvothermal approach of MOF-5 synthesis with a variation of the ligand to zinc ratio from 0.33:1 to 0.75:1 [32]. With an increase in the composition, gradually bigger MOF crystals were achieved along with an elevation in the crystal yield, however, having lower surface areas and poor crystallinity. Here, in the beginning, zinc oxide clusters possessing a particular geometry were supposed to be developed which later formed the tetranuclear super-tetrahedral cluster motif of MOF-5 after reaction with the BDC ligands [20]. Excessive zinc salt could provoke the generation of the zinc oxide cluster prior to the reaction with linkers and thus using a high Zn to linker ratio might be advantageous to avoid the side products.
In another research, McKinstry et al. demonstrated the impact of metal/ligand (M/L) composition with a variation from 3 to 0.67 on the development of MOF-5 [162]. Hence, MOF-5 formed at M/L 1.33 (stoichiometric ratio of the terminal substances) illustrated better crystallinity in comparison with those developed at M/L 3, with narrower Bragg peaks (Figure 5b). Moreover, decreasing the $\mathrm{{M}/L}$ value less than the stoichiometric resulted in excess of the ligand and thus hindered the generation of final MOF products.
3.4.2 Effect of reaction conditions on GOF synthesis:
Effect of temperature:
Srinivas et al. evaluated the influence of temperature on the structure and porosity of GOF-1PBA (Phenyl boronic acid) and GOF14PDBA (B14DBA)[18]. Figure 6a depicts the variation of the XRD results of GO, GOF-1PBA and GOF-14PDBA as a result of tuning the reaction temperature. From the analysis, the best selection of temperature found to achieve the ideal structure of GOFs was about $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Materials synthesized at an elevated temperature than $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ resulted in the continuous reduction of (001) peak and formation of the distinctive (002) graphitic peak similar to reduced GO [166, 167]. Moreover, materials obtained at higher temperatures, as a result of significant drop in the interlayer distance along with the degree of B–O bond development, possessed a noticeable decline in the BET surface area. In brief, the interlayer spacing and BET surface area of GOFs significantly depend on the synthesis temperature.
Effect of reaction solvent:
In accordance with the investigation by Pourazadi et al., finding a suitable solvent for exfoliating GO might result in achieving the highest productivity of the GOF materials [33]. In this manner, the utilization of water, methanol, and DMF as solvents for the dispersion of GO was compared using the absorption spectroscopy. Finally, DMF was depicted as the best solvent especially for generating significantly durable as well as homogeneous dispersion of GO with the maximum absorption capability. The d-spacings of GOFs in water, methanol, and DMF were evaluated as 0.88, 0.85, and 1.01 nm respectively (Figure 6b) which demonstrated the dominance of DMF in achieving the highest value of d-spacing for GOF synthesis since it moved the peak to a reduced value of angle.
Effect of linker concentration:
It might be possible to tune several important aspects of the GOF materials, for example, pore-volume, surface area, and pore size with an appropriate choice of the ligand concentration [17, 18]. Nicolai et al. demonstrated the GOF-n membranes with three distinct linker ratios (the value of n varied from 16, 32 to 64 where n depicted the value of the carbon-to-diboronic acid (DBA) linker ratio or the number of GO carbon in each ligand) [168]. From this experiment, the GOF-64 materials possessed a distinct pore size in comparison with that of the GOF-32 materials, however identical pore spacing. On the other hand, GOF-16 and GOF-32 materials hold different values of pore spacing although pore size was the same. Moreover, for application in desalination, GOF- $\mathbf{\cdotn}$ $(\mathtt{n}\le32$ ) membranes were proposed as the best for the high penetrability of water molecules denoting $100\%$ salt refusal.
3.4.3 Effect of reaction conditions on COF synthesis:
Effect of temperature:
The development of COF materials is also greatly influenced by the synthesis temperature. In accordance with the previous discussion, the synthesis temperature controls the final MOFs crystal structure and the reaction pathway whereas, in the case of GOFs, the BET surface area and interlayer spacing depend on temperature. However, in COFs, the reversibility of the reaction is the factor altered by the reaction temperature.
Figure 7a shows representative STM images of the surface COF (SCOF)-IC1 formed at various temperatures. Liu et al. evaluated the effect of temperature on this material at 110, 120, and $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after synthesizing through the condensation reaction of 1,3,5 tris(4-aminophenyl)benzene (TAPB, 1) on highly oriented pyrolytic graphite (HOPG) with (TPA, 2) [169]. Initially, at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , a minor quantity of structures was generated but the SCOF-IC1 structures went through an oriented growth after the production of the nuclei upon elevation of the reaction temperature. Here, the successful attachment of TPA (2) and TAPB (1) to form SCOF-IC1 is greatly influenced by the evaporation rate of TPA (2) and the diffusion rate of TAPB (1) on the HOPG surface. However, both rates were poor at lower reaction temperatures and thus hindered the formation of large-scale networks. Moreover, according to Le Chatelier’s principle, an increase in reaction temperature intensifies the reversibility of the reaction and thus, the self-repair of materials might be possible at elevated temperatures to produce high-standard SCOFs. However, too high synthesis temperature is detrimental to the generation of COFs, e.g., increasing temperature to $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ led to the formation of only bare small networks which might be attributed to the sublimation of the starting materials, the fragmentation of SCOF-IC1 networks, and/or the unmanageable complex side reactions among TAPB and TPA.
Effect of reaction solvent:
The use of different types of solvents might result in the synthesis of various crystalline COF materials as demonstrated by Dalapati et al. [170]. In the presence of AcOH as a catalyst; o-dichlorobenzene (oDCB), o-DCB/n-BuOH (1.9/0.1 by volume), mesitylene/dioxane ( $1/3$ by volume), and o-DCB/dioxane 1 $3/1$ by volume) were used as solvents to obtain crystalline frameworks (Figure 7b). Among these, the maximum XRD intensity was found with the o-DCB with a BET surface area of $599\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . In summary, reaction conditions play important role in determining some important factors such as the crystallinity, BET surface area, interlayer spacing, and reaction kinetics of the organic framework materials. Hence, both process and compositional parameters have a significant influence on the final framework structures and dimensions, while reaction conditions are believed to play the most sensitive role. For example, an inappropriate condition may lead to unexpected, [171] agglomerated [172] and non-uniform [173, 174] morphologies. For introducing new organic framework materials in this field, it is mandatory to optimize the conditions through trial and error, which is time-consuming. Moreover, as mentioned previously, there is no universal law for picking the reaction conditions and the effect can be different with reactants even in the identical reaction conditions. Besides, it is greatly challenging to identify the appropriate function of all the parameters together in a synthesis reaction. Although plenty of research has been carried out to investigate such effects, it is still difficult to construct predefined structures in an intended manner since each parameter has a unique function in the synthesis of organic framework structures.
3.5 Effect of post-synthesis modification:
## 3.5.1 Post synthesis modification on MOFs
Post-synthesis modification of MOFs has attracted great attention especially for achieving the functionalized materials which are not possible to be synthesized through a straightforward reaction between metal centers and organic linkers [74, 175]. The exchange of metal center [176] or ligand [177, 178]; coordination of a metal center [179] or ligand [180] into a MOF, and the generation or fragmentation of a covalent bond are the desirable ways of these modifications. Post-synthesis modification reactions might happen through incomplete or absolute conversion of MOFs where the degree of conversion is controllable by changing the steric bulk of the reactant as well as through regulating the amount of the reactive groups available on the MOFs.
Moussa et al. investigated the post-synthesis modification of UiO-67-bpy MOF with copper(II) bromide using the mechanochemical method [181]. In this method, the chemical reactions were conducted by grinding in the presence of liquid with slight incorporation of methanol or DMF. Afterward, the insoluble materials were retrieved and washed with methanol and DMF to eliminate the unreacted substances. Further analysis of the dissolved MOF with ICP-AES revealed the perfect coordination of the bipyridine linker sites with copper to generate a functional MOF, $\left[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{6}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mathrm{CuBr}_{2})_{6}\right]$ . However, a decline in the BET surface area was revealed $(155~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ instead of $650~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ before coordination) which might result in decreasing the capability of the material to adsorb nitrogen due to the fulfillment of the pores by inclusion of copper (II) bromide on each bipyridine site of UiO-67-bpy (Figure 8).
On the other hand, Abdelhameed et al. studied the post-synthesis modification of a porous iron MOF with chemically crosslinked EDTA (ethylene diaminetetra-acetic dianhydride) to modify the pore size of the MOF materials [182]. Ferrous chloride was used as the source of the metal cation and 2- aminoterephthalic acid as the organic linker. The resultant Fe-MOF was activated through the exchange of the guest solvent with dry methanol which was then evacuated at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Afterward, EDTA was consolidated into the Fe-MOF through chemical cross-linking. However, further characterization with SEM and PXRD of the materials verified that the as-synthesized Fe-MOF was unaffected and the material was able to withhold stability even after post-synthesis modification.
In another investigation, Gadipelli et al. conducted the post-synthesis modification of MOF-5 by thermal annealing at $350{-}500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under the flow of $\Nu_{2}$ [183]. Hence, the annealing formed a defective structure with a local vacancy but kept the original long-range orientation and the crystallinity of the material intact. This vacancy served as defect-generated active sites of the ligand as well as the unsaturated metal node performed as powerful binding sites for adsorption of various guest substances. Moreover, the preservation of the MOF structure having a high specific surface area along with the micropore volume concurrently enhanced the molecular adsorption. However, the MOFs achieved after post-synthesis modification demonstrated high strength towards uptaking cyclic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ as well as air/moisture.
3.5.2 Effect of post-synthesis modification on COFs:
The post-synthesis modification has been extensively utilized for the functionalization of COFs, especially by metalation [184-191]. For instance, Ma et al. illustrated the customization of sulfur derivatives on a vinyl-functionalized mesoporous 2D COF (COF-V) by thiol−ene “click” reaction. Typically, the modification was done through the reaction of COF-V with 1,2-ethanedithiol, resulting in the synthesis of COF-S-SH (Figure 9) with a high absorption capacity for $\mathrm{Hg^{2+}}$ [185].
Figure 9: Post synthesis modification of COF-V to synthesize COF-S-SH by thiol-ene click reaction, reproduced from ref. [185] with permission from ACS, copyright 2017.
Hence, the final material has a great affinity towards mercury with dense at the same time accessible and flexible chelating parts which altogether led to the outstanding performance. Besides, McGrier et al. demonstrated the synthesis and post metalation of DBA–3D–COF with nickel which possessed enhanced absorption efficiencies toward ethane and ethylene [192]. Moreover, Wang et al reported an improvement in catalytic efficiency of COFs using post-synthesis metalation [186].
In another experiment by Shi et al., HCOF or hierarchical COF spheres were synthesized and metallated with $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ [193]. The post-synthesis metalation changed the color of the final brown materials to black although the structure and morphology of the HCOFs didn’t change at all. Moreover, this modification led to the formation of metallated HCOF, an excellent photothermal agent (PTA) for photothermal therapy (PTT). Thus, the post-synthesis metalation strategy demonstrated significant prospective in tuning the features of COFs and making them appropriate for diversified applications.
4. Tuning effect on properties of organic framework materials:
4.1 Tuning effect on electronic properties of organic framework materials:
4.1.1 Tuning of electronic properties of MOFs:
As mentioned previously, MOFs are synthesized through connecting fixed metal cations to the organic units. Similar to the linkers, the rigid metal ions become redox-inactive substances after their coordination. Therefore, due to the unavailability of free change carriers as well as low-energy routes for charge transfer, these materials usually possess insulating features with poor conductivities $(<10^{-}$ $^{10}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ ). However, the conductivity can be improved by employing particular approaches, e.g., the inclusion of guest substances, doping with redox-active molecules, and hybridization with other polymers having good conductivity, etc. Moreover, a careful choice of metal ions and linkers can modify the conductivity of MOFs [194].
For generating MOFs with good conducting properties, both the charge density and movability should be necessarily strong enough. Charge density depends on the amount of loosely attached charge carriers $(>10^{15}\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ which can be free like in metallic conductors or can be activated through heating, similar to that of semiconductors. In the case of MOFs, the charge carriers can be provided by the metal ions as well as the organic units. Hence, the metal ions should contain rich-energy electrons or holes, as in the unpaired electron of square-planar $\mathrm{d^{9}C u^{I I}}$ centers or the minority-spin electron of highspin octahedral $\mathrm{d}^{6}\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}}$ centers. Besides, to allow easier charge transfer through metal ions or nodes, the organic units need to be stable radicals for supplying lone pair electrons or redox-active substances. For example, Nickel 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaiminotriphenylene and Cobalt 2,3,6,7,10,11- triphenylenehexathiolate are examples of the electronically conductive 2D MOFs [195, 196]. This phenomenon can be attributed to the fact that metals can transmit effective 2D conjugation pathways between electroactive organic compounds and thus produce new materials with remarkable electrical properties. Moreover, the conductivity of MOFs depends on some external factors, e.g., guest molecules and film morphology.
Kuc et al. presented a synopsis of the electronic properties of a group of IRMOFs with an investigation of their charge distribution [197]. These materials, due to an extensive range of the bandgap (1-5.5 eV), can be listed as semiconductors or insulators. Here, the $\displaystyle\mathbf{sp}^{2}$ states of the C from organic units controlled the highest occupied molecular orbital-lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (HOMOLUMO) bands, in the neighborhood of the Fermi level. Due to the contribution of $\pi$ states, the bandgap decreased as a result of increasing the number of the $\displaystyle\mathbf{sp}^{2}$ carbon atoms in the ligand. Moreover, the partial density of states (PDOS) of carbon and zinc atoms were found to remain similar in ligands as well as in the expanded MOF crystals (Figures 10a and 10b). Therefore, the electronic properties were the same for both MOF crystals as well as building units.
Tang et al. strategically analyzed the electronic as well as charge transfer characteristics of 2D MOF semiconducting materials $\mathrm{(M}_{3}S_{6}C_{6},$ , $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}$ , Ca, Zn, Cd, Ge, and Sn) [198]. With an increase in strength of the p-d coupling, the efficient mass significantly decreased from group IIA (Mg, Ca) to IVA (Ge, Sn) to IIB (Zn, Cd) (no coupling) MOFs and thus p-d coupling was found as the major aspect for evaluating the hole transport mechanisms. Moreover, for further understanding of these results, the band structure and PDOS of these materials were determined. In the case of a hole, the valence band maximum (VBM) of the systems was actually controlled by the p orbitals of the S and C atoms. In comparison with the group IVA (Ge, Sn) and IIA (Mg, Ca)-substituted MOFs, $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{3}S_{6}C_{6}}}}$ and ${\mathrm{Cd}}_{3}{\mathrm{S}}_{6}{\mathrm{C}}_{6}$ featured the maximum valence band distribution near the band edges which might be due to the strongest p-d coupling between the group IIB (Zn, Cd) d orbitals and anion p orbitals. On the other hand, Zn and Cd had comparatively shallow d orbitals in energy and thus possessed little gap in energy $\left(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{p}}-\varepsilon_{\mathrm{d}}\right)$ for the group IIB (Zn, Cd) d orbitals and anion p orbitals.
The interpretation and tuning of the electronic properties of MOFs are the major elemental questions that might reveal the employability of these materials for several applications e.g., supercapacitors, electrodes, “smart” membranes, and thermoelectric devices. In this regard, one of the difficulties can be the synthesis of conductive hybrid substances with the ability to secure framework crystallinity except obstructing MOF pores. Some previous studies have already shown that a MOF matrix can take in another metal with preserving crystallinity [75, 176, 199-204]. Thus, electronic properties can be tailored via the immobilization of “guest metal” inside MOF metal nodes. From this perspective, a study was particularly attentive to the basic factors that contributed to the electronic properties of three different types of bimetallic systems, $\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{x-y}}\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{y}}^{\prime}$ -MOFs, $\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{y}}^{\prime}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}\mathbf{s}$ , and $\mathbf{M}_{\mathbf{x}}$ (ligand- ${\bf{\cdot}}{\bf{M}}_{\mathrm{{y}}}^{\prime}$ )-MOFs ( $\mathbf{M}^{\prime}$ is a second metal) [205]. According to the findings, not only the metal node engineering but also the occupancy of unsaturated metal parts and the topology of the framework might significantly control the density of electronic states (DOS) in the neighborhood of the Fermi edge.
