For example, in the bimetallic Zr/Co-based MOFs, the tuning of the electronic structure depends on the site selected for the coordination of the second metal [205]. Moreover, monometallic Cu and Znbased, and bimetallic Cu/Zn-based MOFs show insulating characteristics instead of bimetallic cobaltcontaining MOFs possesses semiconducting features. While analyzing the DOS in the monometallic Cu-BTC material, the DOS near EF (conduction band) emerges from the p orbitals of O whereas the
DOS above EF consists of d orbitals from Cu. On the other hand, the inclusion of cobalt results in a decrease in the band gap of $0.6\mathrm{eV}$ due to the dominance by cobalt d-orbitals into DOS near EF.
4.1.2 Tuning of electronic properties of GOFs:
The electronic properties of GOFs such as the bandgap can be controlled through a proper selection of the organic spacer as well as its concentration. GOFs are supposed to show greatly modifiable metallic to semiconducting features [206]. The electronic properties of three GOFs are based on different pillar concentrations (GOF-8, GOF-16, and GOF-32) and two distinct ligands B14DBA or B14PDBA and benzene-4,4-diboronic acid or B44BPDBA) were evaluated by the density functional theory (DFT) calculation and Vienna abinitio simulation package [207, 208]. Moreover, Zhu et al. evaluated the band gaps with full DOS calculations and verified the reliability of bandgap measurement through band structures [206]. As shown in Figure. 11, increase in pillar concentration of GOF structure resulted in a larger band gap and this tendency didn’t change even after the modification of the organic unit. Therefore, the electronic properties of GOFs are tunable through varying the concentration of pillar unit.
4.1.3 Tuning of electronic Properties of COFs:
Zhu et al. studied the electronic properties and the impact of strain on the electronic properties of 2D COFs through combining the DFT and quasiparticle theory calculations [209]. The characteristics of COFs were investigated with various sizes as well as chemical compositions. The bandgap was shown to be gradually smaller with increasing the chain length and therefore the electronic transport properties of COFs might be controlled with a variation of the chain length (Figures 12a and 12b). The results also demonstrated that the use of graphene substrates for the synthesis of COFs did not significantly alter their properties.
In brief, the tailoring of the electronic properties of 2D COFs, for example, the bandgap can be achieved via the proper tuning of the structure, especially through enhancing the number of organic chain links [209]. On the other hand, considering 3D COFs, the energy band gaps have been found in the range of $2.3{\-}4.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which reveals the semiconducting nature of these materials [210], similar to those of hexagonal 2D COFs [211].
