4.2 Tuning effect on mechanical properties of organic framework materials:
4.2.1 Tuning of mechanical properties of MOFs:
MOFs show a change in structure upon application of hydrostatic pressures which is not only practically relevant but also fundamentally interesting. Hence, along with the plentiful diversification of MOF structure, it is important to set up the design rules to further understand and improve the mechanical stability of these materials.
As mentioned earlier, MOFs are highly porous with low densities, although some theoretical predictions have given impressive values for elastic constants, shear strength, and hardness [212]. A study conducted by Bundschuh and his group evaluated Young’s modulus of high-quality HKUST-1, a Cu-MOF thin film with a value of 9.3 GPa which is considerably higher than organic polymers with similar hardness. Moreover, as shown in figure-13, both hardness and indentation modulus has been found to increase with an increase in indentation depth [213].
Voorde et al. investigated the mechanical properties of Cu-based MOFs with three different ligands, $\mathrm{Cu(CHDA)}$ , $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{INA})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ (CHDA $\mathbf{\Sigma}=$ trans-cyclohexane-1,4-dicarboxylate and $\mathrm{{INA}=}$ isonicotinate) [214]. Among those, $\mathrm{Cu(CHDA)}$ pointed to the strong substrate attachment strength and became the hardest and stiffest material holding good elastic recoveries as well as abrasion resistance which might be due to the $\mathrm{Cu}$ -oxo chain flowing through the c-axis of the crystal lattice. In addition, the flexible nature of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{INA})_{2}$ along with breathing behavior contributed to the extremely low Young's modulus. On the other hand, relatively weak coordination bonding of the pyridine ring, which is supposed to be more prone to the fragmentation of bonds in comparison with the strong ionic and covalent linkages, leads to the low hardness of the same material.
Kuc et al. reported the mechanical properties of a group of MOFs using a density functional-based tight-binding method [197]. For survey, the cubic array of $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{4}O}(C O_{2})_{6}}}$ units coordinated through distinct organic ligands was selected. The frameworks were stable with bulk moduli enhancement (0.5 $-24\mathrm{GPa})$ ) while reducing the size of the organic ligand. Moreover, the bulk moduli might also depend on the structures of MOFs since the FCC (face-centered cubic) structure had a smaller bulk-moduli than the BCC (body-centered cubic) systems.
4.2.2 Tuning of mechanical properties of GOFs:
Mechanical strain might be a powerful and nonchemical manner to modify the band gaps of GOF-Ln materials. Zhu et al. studied the impact of strain on the electronic properties of GOF14PDBA-8,16 by first-principles DFT calculations [206]. Here, the tensile strain would be liable for the fragmentation of bonds between the layers of graphene, and then the weakened graphene’s electronic features dominate the overall electronic characteristics of GOFs. The direct strain influenced the band gap of GOF-14PDBA-8 in a more intense manner than that of GOF-14PDBA-16 (Figure 14a). The Young’s modulus of GOF-14PDBA-8 and GOF-14PDBA-16 were calculated as 350 GPa and 137 GPa, respectively. Moreover, the mechanical stability of these materials was evaluated based on the reaction upon shear strain (Figure 14b). The shear modulus was calculated for GOF-14PDBA-8 $\mathrm{(G=8.8\GPa)}$ and GOF-14PDBA-16 $\mathrm{\DeltaG}=2.0\mathrm{GPa}\$ ), putting a conclusion that the ligands consumed major strain and became the limiting factors for generating the mechanical stability of GOFs under shear strain.
Figure 14: (a) Dark red: Deviation of band gap $\mathrm{(E_{g})}$ as a function of direct strain for i. GOF-14PDBA8, ii. GOF-14PDBA-16. Purple: Internal energy with respect to direct strain. The positive part of the direct strain represents enlargement whereas the negative shows constriction; (b) Dark red: as a function of shear strain for i. GOF-14PDBA-8, ii. GOF-14PDBA-16. Purple: Internal energy with respect to shear strain. The positive and negative values of shear strain depict reverse directions. (a-b) have been reproduced from ref. [206] with permission from ACS, copyright 2013.
Another article presented a comparative study on the impact of the sonication of graphite oxide (GiO) on the creation of the mechanical characteristics of the terminating GOF materials [215]. Hence, a mild sonication was found to yield a large amount of large GO sheets with an adequate balance of great strength and extensive elongation and thus revealed a remarkable toughness $\mathrm{1}.09{\pm}0.14\mathrm{MJm^{-3}}$ ).
On the other hand, lengthening of the sonication time caused a reduction in the GO sizes which led to deterioration in the mechanical properties of GOFs.
4.2.3 Tuning of mechanical properties of COFs:
COFs have shown an improved mechanical property compared with other polymeric materials [216, 217]. Hence, AFM nanoindentation has been an established method to evaluate the nanomechanical property of 2D inorganic substances such as graphene, $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{WSe}_{2}$ [216, 218, 219]. Hao et al. synthesized free-standing COF films and adapted the Atomic Force Microscope (AFM) nanoindentation technique to measure their mechanical properties. The COFs possessed good mechanical characteristics with an elastic modulus of $119.1\pm2.9\mathrm{Nm}^{-1}$ and Young’s modulus of 25.9 $\pm0.6$ GPa [217].
Zhu et al. investigated the impact of steady strain on the electronic properties of COF-1 with the firstprinciples DFT theory [209]. The dissimilarity of the unit cell total energy upon strain had an almost parabolic feature and thus suggested the agreement of Hooke’s law within small-scale strain. Moreover, the tensile strain caused a decrease in the band gap whereas compressive strain increased the band gap provided ε is smaller than $-2\%$ .
On the other hand, Lukose et al. calculated the mechanical characteristics of 3D COFs with an application of (DFTB) level of theory [210]. The findings proposed that the mechanical stability of COFs might depend on the topology since carbon nitride (ctn) nets had shown mechanically more stability compared to boracite or bor. In the case of interpenetrated COFs, the maximum bulk modulus was 20 GPa whereas non-interpenetrated COF-300 possessed a poorer bulk modulus with a value of 3.17 GPa, suggesting that interpenetration could accommodate further mechanical stability (Table-3).
4.3 Tuning effect on chemical properties of organic framework materials:
4.3.1 Tuning of chemical properties of MOFs:
The chemical stability of MOFs mostly depends on liquid water and water vapor [220]. The chemical weak points of MOFs lie in the nodes or metal-ligand coordination which upon hydrolysis results in a protonated ligand as well as a hydroxide or water ligated node. The presence of acidic solutions speeds up the generation of the hydroxide ligated one whereas the basic characteristic solutions lead to the production of the water ligated nodes.
In the case of Zr-MOFs, the Zr-oxo cluster and carboxylate linker form a strong coordination bond and thus contribute to the ultra-high chemical stabilities of these materials. Especially, the quaternization of the pyridyl groups modifies the electronic structure of the organic linker and gives scopes to tailor the chemical properties of the consequent MOFs. For example, Xu et al. demonstrated that the chemical properties of free pyridyl-containing MOFs could be modified through N-quaternization post-synthesis modification [221]. The resultant UiO-67-bpy-Me (bpy $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 2,2bipyridine- $^{.4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{.}}$ - dicarboxylic acid, Me $\mathbf{\Sigma}=$ methyl) maintained good crystallinity as well as chemical stabilities in the commonly used organic solvents compared to the initial UiO-67-bpy MOF. However, the modified material possessed decreased stability when water was used as a solvent which was attributed to the weaker coordination of the $Z\mathrm{r}{-}\mathrm{O}$ cluster with N-quaternized bpy ligand. The $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ -quaternization made the parent UiO-67-bpy MOF cationic and thus resulted in declining the electron concentrations of the carboxylic oxygen atoms on the ligand and lessening the strength of the $z_{\mathrm{r-O}}$ coordination bonds. Consequently, compared to its parent, UiO-67-bpy-Me became more prone to being deteriorated in presence of water molecules.
Another study assembled GO and copper-centered MOF in order to form a composite which possessed great response when employed as a tri‐functional catalyst in the hydrogen evolution (HER), oxygen evolution (OER), and oxygen reduction (ORR) electrocatalysis reactions [222]. The composites exhibited smaller overpotentials (-0.209, 1.34, -0.03V Vs reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) respectively for HER, OER, and ORR) at higher currents (122.48, 5.8, and $5.32\mathrm{\mAcm^{-2}}$ respectively for HER, OER, and ORR) in the reactions and became more stable in acid medium relative to the pure
MOFs. Here, the distinctive porous scaffold assembly, better charge transfer, and combined interactions of GO and the MOF led to the improvement in electrocatalytic features as well as the acid stability of GO-MOF composite.
Babu et al. investigated solid Fe(BTC) MOFs and found that precise and reversible $\mathrm{Fe(III/II)}$ reduction reactions happened in the presence of aqueous acid upon immobilization onto platinum electrode instead of graphite [223]. However, no significant voltammetric response was shown while Fe(BTC) was dipped into aqueous $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ potassium chloride irrespective of the choice of the electrode. Here, the reduction and re-oxidation of Fe(III/II) were reversible having a value of $\mathrm{E_{mid}{=}0}.48\:\mathrm{V}$ vs. saturated calomel electrode (SCE). The midpoint potential didn’t vary with the concentration of hydrochloric acid (HCl) and thus not likely to be related to the inclusion of proton into the ${\mathrm{Fe(BTC)}}$ system rather might be associated with the reductive dissolution which was previously observed for $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{BTC})}_{2}$ MOF [224].
On the other hand, under the same conditions, the cyclic voltammograms of F (BTC) revealed significantly decreased reactivity at the surface of the basal plane pyrolytic graphite electrode along with a broader distance between peaks. In comparison with platinum, graphite surfaces are supposed to possess decelerated electron transport for the reduction of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ to $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ [225] and other ironcontaining minerals [226]. Here, the electron transport rate from graphite to the immobilized Fe(BTC) microcrystals might be the prime limiting factor which could be operated by the potential.
Moreover, immobilization onto platinum electrode instead of graphite and dipping into $0.5~\mathrm{M}$ HCl resulted in significantly reversible and stable cyclic voltammetric responses for $\mathrm{Fe(BTC)}$ over many cycles of potential. The analysis of the impact of the potential scan rate on the peak currents towards reduction and oxidation demonstrated a “steady-state” characteristic with basically constant peak current over scan rates of 0.01 to $0.15~\mathrm{Vs}^{-1}$ which is not usual. Here, the current response might be limited by the slow dissolution of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and thus led to an entirely dynamically restricted system.
The simplified process for the reduction of Fe–BTC can be shown as a reductive disintegration (according to Eqs.(i) and (ii)) [223]:
$\mathrm{Fe\(III)\(BTC)\(solid)+3H^{+}\ (a q)\ -s e^{3+}\ (a q)+H_{3}B T C\ (a q)...................}.$ $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\left(\mathrm{aq}\right)+\mathrm{e}^{-}\xrightarrow{}\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\left(\mathrm{aq}\right).................\left(\mathrm{ii}\right)$
4.3.2 Tuning of chemical properties of GOFs:
The accessible surface area and stability of GOFs mostly depend on the reaction temperature and the variety and length of the pillaring unit [18]. Pourazadi et al. synthesized GOF by incorporating boronic acid into a GO structure with an enhanced electrochemical performance [33]. In the ORR, the analysis revealed that GOFs preferred a $4\mathrm{e^{-}}$ reduction mechanism. The electrocatalytic activities of GO and
GOF were investigated by CV and rotating disk electrode (RDE) analysis. The CV of GOF had a remarkable increase in the cathodic current due to faster electron transport compared to the GO which could be attributed to the synergistic impacts of the enhancement in surface area for $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ adsorption as well as the increased electron-accepting–donating potential of boron in each of the oxygen molecule [222, 227]. Here, a positively charged B was generated for attracting oxygen due to the difference in the electronegativity of B, O, and C (in $\mathrm{CBO}_{2}$ ). Moreover, boron might perform as an electron supplier for the reduction of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ through sharing p electrons of C-C to the empty $2\mathrm{Pz}$ orbital [228].
4.3.3 Tuning of chemical properties of COFs:
The nature of the covalent linkages joining the molecular building blocks denotes the crystallinity and the physical and chemical properties of COFs [105]. Khattak et al. presented a 2D COF, Tapa-Py consisting of redox-active pyridine sections possessing reversible electrochemical operations [229]. Upon application as an electrode in supercapacitors, it demonstrated faradaic characteristics and outstanding chemical stability even after 6000 charge-discharge cycles.
Moreover, while comparing the CV curves of TaPa-Py with another COF without a pyridine unit, namely, diaminopyridine-triformylphloroglucinol (DAB-TFP) COF at a specific potential, the voltammetric current density of the former one was considerably better than the latter one, recommending an improvement in response upon the addition of the pyridine unit. Moreover, at electrode potentials negative to 0.4V, the pyridine active COF possessed a greater total specific capacitance at a high scan rate $(139.4~\mathrm{Fg^{-1}}$ at $200\ \mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ ) compared to the other one $(31~\mathrm{Fg^{-1}}$ at 200 $\mathrm{{\mV{s^{-1}}}}$ ). Here, according to the electrochemical measurements, the pyridine units demonstrated faradaic characteristics after going through a reversible redox reaction and thus resulted in a higher specific capacitance in comparison with its electroactive monomer as well as another COF unoccupying redox-active substances. Moreover, the double-layer capacitive nature of the DAB-TFP COFs having smaller surface area and lacking pyridine units suggested the generation of the redox characteristic of the TaPa-Py COF from its redox-active pyridine units.
Chandra et al. synthesized two COFs namely, Tp-Azo and Tp-Stb from the Schiff base reaction of triformylphloroglucinol (Tp) and 4,4′azodianiline (Azo) or $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -diaminostilbene (Stb) respectively [230]. In boiling water, both of the COFs were observed to maintain stability, crystallinity, and porosity for several days. Moreover, these materials demonstrated stability in strong acid (9N HCl) at the same time almost maintenance of the molecular crystallinity. The BET surface areas of the activated TpAzo and Tp-Stb materials were evaluated as 1328 and $422{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ respectively (Figure 15). The smaller surface area of the latter one might be related to the poor solubility of Stb in organic solvents which led to the lower crystallinity as well as channels lacking proper uniformity of Tp-Stb COFs.
## 4.4 Tuning effect on thermal properties of organic framework materials
4.4.1 Tuning of thermal properties of MOFs:
The thermal degradation of MOFs happens upon breaking the bond between node and ligand with or afterward ligand combustion. Therefore, the thermal stability of these materials is mostly connected to the strength of the node-ligand bond as well as the number of ligands associated per node. Hosne et al. introduced thermally stable porous MOFs with a simple method of preparation [231]. The study derived four mononuclear and hetero-binuclear materials as a result of the reaction between zinc nitrate or a mixture of zinc and cobalt nitrates and 1,4bis[(3,5-dimethyl)pyrazole-4-yl]benzene $\mathrm{\langleH_{2}D P B\rangle}$ ) using ethanol (compound 1 and 3) or dimethylformamide (compound 2 and 4) as solvents in solvothermal approach. Here, compound 1 was found as the most stable whereas compound 2 was the worst since these (1), (2), (3) and (4) materials after activation, demonstrated a decrease in $10\%$ of their masses at 387, 285, 376 and $326^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively.
The thermal stability of a Cu-BTC MOF was analyzed by Schlichte et al. and found that the material might undergo decomposition at a higher reaction temperature (62 K) [232]. The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) results revealed $32\%$ continuous deterioration in mass for the dehydration of the assynthesized materials up to 523K. The change in weight for 3, 6, and $9\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules in each $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}$ unit was evaluated as $8.2\%$ , $15.2\%$ , and $21.1\%$ , respectively which could depend on the pre-treatment of the material. The mass degradation was $1-2\%$ for materials dried in the high vacuum ( $1\times10^{-4}$ mbar, 373K) which was however $12.2\%$ after drying in the air (363K). The decomposition of the network occurred at 623K even though the weight loss of the material was not significant.
Sofi et al. reported the microwave-assisted synthesis and TGA results of an iron-based MOF composite with $\mathrm{Ag/Ag_{3}P O_{4}}$ [233]. The functionalization of MIL-53(Fe) MOF with $\mathrm{Ag/Ag_{3}P O_{4}}$ led to better thermal stability since the association of the MOF with silver nanoparticles resulted in greater strength of the organic ligand coordinated to iron. In the temperature range of $280{-}600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the MIL-53(Fe) MOF underwent $40\%$ degradation which was however $22\%$ after functionalization suggesting high thermal stability of the composite material.
Moreover, another investigation had shown that owing to the inherent design, IRMOFs possessed interesting thermal characteristics. Despite having exceptional adsorption properties, these materials consistently possessed negative thermal-expansion coefficients and were the most constricted substances found till then [234]. The phenomenon might be attributed to a local influence of enlarging the bond length upon heating and a long-range impact resulting in a smaller average distance among edges with temperature due to the thermal movement of the ligand molecules.
4.4.2 Tuning of thermal properties of GOFs:
The thermal stability of GOFs depends on the pillaring unit since the use of boronic acid results in an increase in the thermal strength provided by the strong boronate-ester bonds between GO layers. Srinivas et al conducted the TGA of GOF materials with different ligands up to $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using nitrogen atmosphere [18]. In the case of GO, a prompt weight loss was found with increasing temperature to approximately $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and became the most unstable material among these. Here, the prime reduction in weight in the range of $150{-}200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was related to the production of carbon dioxide and water from the fragmentation of the oxygenated chemical groups whereas the earlier trend before $125^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was associated with the evaporation of the stacked water molecules. Finally, a plateau was revealed after $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ showing $48\%$ of the total weight loss. On the other hand, initially, GOFs demonstrated remarkable weight loss at a temperature less than $125^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with comparatively better stability of about $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ than GO. Most importantly, the degradation temperature of GOFs didn’t vary upon modification of ligands. Moreover, the inclusion of carbon and boron from organic spacers enhanced the mass among the graphene layers and thus the final weight loss of GOFs was minimized compared to GO.
4.4.3 Tuning of thermal properties of COFs:
COFs are supposed to possess poor thermal conductivity because of lacking high densities (e.g. 0.17 $\mathrm{gcm}^{-3}$ for COF-108) and plenty of vacant spaces in structures [104]. The cage-like construction of these materials, likely laced, results in bridges through connecting the pores and therefore is able to set the sum of energy that can be transferred in the microscale range. These features have provided the COFs and the associative substances with outstanding potential for application as thermal insulators. On the other hand, COFs are made of strong covalent bonds generated from connecting light-weight components and thus possess high thermal stabilities. There are several studies regarding the analysis of the thermal properties of COFs. For example, Freitas et al investigated the dependence of thermal conductivity on the pore structure and surface area of several COF materials namely COF-300, RIO1, RIO-4, and RIO-20 [235]. The pore size was revealed as the major factor to generate the conductivity of these materials with thermal conductivity values found in the range of 0.038 to $0.048~\mathrm{W~m^{-1}~K^{-1}}$ . Pores consist of vacant areas and would be packed with gas or liquid flows (e.g., air). Therefore, an increase in the size of the pores resulted in a decrease in the heat transfer throughout the material since these are non-metallic frameworks. Among the four COFs mentioned above, COF-300 had a moderately greater value of thermal conductivity along with the best crystallinity due to its catenated wall construction which added several pathways for heat transfer via phonos in the interlaced structure.
5. Tuning effect on applications of organic framework materials:
Similar to the influences of tuning strategies on properties of organic framework materials, it is also possible to tune their performances through the modification of structure, surface area, porosity, and crystallinity via synthesis with different linkers at different reaction conditions. To date, these materials have been implemented in diversified applications and the performance greatly depends on the type and length of the ligand, synthesis temperature, time, and composition. In the following sub-sections, the tuning effects on catalytic, desalination, gas storage, and gas sensing applications of organic framework materials are discussed. Moreover, various synthesis routes are available for generating organic framework materials aiming at different types of applications. A summary of such synthesis methods for targeted applications is shown in Table 4.
5.1 Tuning effect on the catalytic applications of organic framework materials:
Catalysis is one of the most distinct and dynamic applications of organic framework materials. The bulk density, remarkable stability, and plenty of catalytically active sites have made them useful for chemical, photocatalytic and electrocatalytic applications [33, 236-240]. Especially, the architectural engineering and post-synthesis approaches to MOFs provide space to incorporate functional components and thus enhance the catalytic competence in various organic transformations [241-243].
Playing with the synthesis reaction conditions can be a suitable way to optimize the catalytic performance of MOFs. In an investigation, Jiang et. al. investigated the effect of reaction temperature on Co-MOF-74 for the low-temperature $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ -SCR (selective catalytic reduction) process [244], in which MOFs were synthesized at 100, 120, 135, and $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using a solvothermal approach. MOFs synthesized at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ demonstrated a unique flower shape morphology with the highest surface area $\mathrm{(928.9\m^{2}/g)}$ ) and pore volume $(0.4296~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ , whereas materials synthesized at higher temperatures possessed surface area of 848.8, 816.8 and $529.6~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and pore volume of 0.4157, 0.4045, 0.2872 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ respectively. Therefore, Co-MOF-74 synthesized at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ illustrated the optimized catalytic performances with $68\%$ NO conversion compared to 50, 53, and $52\%$ from structures synthesized at 120, 135, and $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ respectively. Other possible routes to modulate the catalytic efficiency of MOFs are tuning morphology and electronic structure [245], tailoring Lewis acidity [246], doping and forming composites [247], and strain engineering [248]. Besides, the catalytic activity of GOFs can be improved by incorporating noble metals such as Pd into the structure [249].
A recent study suggests that tuning of electronic properties through bandgap engineering can be a possible strategy to improve the photocatalytic performance of COFs. Singh et. al. designed four types of COF-based photocatalysts from reaction with amine and aldehyde for visible-light-induced solar fuel production from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ [250]. Hence, a variation of the number of hydroxyl units (0-3) in the aldehyde precursor leads to the formation of COFs with a fine-tuning of bandgap energies and band positions with dissimilar surface areas. Final materials from 0, 1, 2, and 3 hydroxyl group-containing precursors possessed surface areas of 816, 1010, 1660, and $1056~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and bandgap energies of 2.65, 2.05, 1.95, and $2.40~\mathrm{eV}$ respectively. Among these, the COF with two hydroxyl units demonstrated significantly enhanced catalytic performance of solar light-driven 1,4-NADH (reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) regeneration up to $94.6\%$ towards $226.3~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ formic acid in $90~\mathrm{min}$ . Such an improved catalytic performance might be attributed to the combining factors of having a fully conjugated system, the most suitable bandgap, optimized band edge positions, higher crystallinity, and unique coral-like morphology. Moreover, another way to modulate the catalytic activity of COFs can be via nanoparticle encapsulation [251].
5.2 Tuning effect on the desalination applications of organic framework materials:
There are several factors influencing the implementation of MOFs for desalination applications such as water stability, type of metal ions and linkers, pore size, functionalization, dispersibility, and particle size [254]. For example, Ou et. al. developed a photoresponsive MIL-53 MOF upon functionalization with poly (spiropyran acrylate) (PSP) as an energy-efficient and sustainable adsorbent material for rapid desalination application[255]. The MOF-PSP material could yield $139.51\mathrm{kg^{-1}d a y^{-1}}$ fresh waterconsuming $0.11\mathrm{KWhm}^{-3}$ of energy for desalining 2,233 ppm synthetic brackish water with remarkable stability and cycling performance. On the other hand, the hydrophobicity of graphene and tunable ligand concentration of GOFs gave these materials combined advantages of good penetrability of water and mighty filtration. Nicolai et. al. studied the desalination performances of GOFs using MD simulation with a variation in thickness $2.5{\sim}6.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) as well as ligand concentration (n) of 16, 32, 64 [168]. Herein, $\mathrm{n}{=}16$ and 32 GOFs manifested $100\%$ salt refusal in RO, whereas the GOF-64 membranes, despite having excellent water permeability, failed to exhibit an ideal desalination performance $(6.1\mathrm{-}49.1\%$ ). Nevertheless, according to the simulation results, using GOF-64 membranes with larger thicknesses $(\geq100\mathrm{nm})$ might be a solution for improving desalination efficiency.
5.3 Tuning effect on the gas sensing applications of organic framework materials:
Over the past few decades, the evolution of gas sensing devices to detect and capture environmentally toxic, hazardous, and volatile organic compounds (VOCs) has attracted increasing attention, primarily due to the remarkable progression in technological advancements in the gas sensing field. MOFs, due to their outstanding characteristics such as plenty of adsorption sites for gas molecules, high porosity, tunable morphologies, structural diversities, and ability of room temperature sensing; have gained enormous attention for gas sensing applications [5, 256]. On the other hand, the modifiable chemical functionality, catalytic performance, absorbability, wettability, electron-rich linker groups, highly accessible active sites, etc. have made COFs a promising and attractive material in sensing applications [257].
Functionalization of ligands might be a potential strategy to improve the gas sensing performance of MOFs. Very recently, Wang et. al. introduced such a strategy with functionalized UIO-66 Zr-MOF [258]. The preliminary UIO-66 MOF was functionalized with benzimidazole, 1,2,4-triazole, methyl imidazole, and amino groups where all the MOFs except UIO-66-(1,2,4-triazole) showed similar responses to detect gaseous aniline. Here, UIO-66-(1,2,4-triazole) had the strongest acidic characteristics based on acidic coefficient value and thereby hold the advantage of having the best interaction with basic aniline which led to the best sensing characteristics of this material. Besides, the formation of a nanocomposite (FeMOFC) from Fe-MOF through the incorporation of a degenerately doped plasmonic metal oxide (e.g. hydrogen doped molybdenum oxide $\left(\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}\right)$ ) may improve the room temperature gas sensing performance of the original Fe-MOF [247]. Herein, the FeMOFC manifested an almost three times larger response towards $\mathsf{1~p p m~N O}_{2}$ with near-full reversibility in comparison with that of FeMOF mainly due to the electron-rich surface of $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ . The electron deficiency of the metal centers results in the interaction between target gas and MOFs. While exposed to the $\Nu{\cal O}_{2}$ gas environment, the charge transfer between FeMOF and the surface adsorbed gas molecules increases the hole concentration in the MOF and thus enhances the electrical conductivity. However, when incorporated with electron-rich $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ , the physisorption of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ gas generates surface dipoles with the Drude-model-like electrons in $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ , creating holes in the structure. The resultant hole conduction paths between FeMOF and $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ further increase the electrical conductivity of FeMOFC.
5.4 Tuning effect on the gas storage applications of organic framework materials:
Gas capture and storage are important for environmental protection and energy utilization. For example, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is responsible for soaring the greenhouse effect and thereby environmental pollution. Similarly, other gases such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ are essential for the extensive use of clean energy. Moreover, the separation and storage of toxic gases, such as CO and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , are crucial for mitigating environmental pollution and the synthesis of industrial chemicals. In this manner, organic framework materials comprising massive and tunable porous structures as well as large surface areas have found notable attention to capture and store plenty of different types of gas molecules in the void spaces [259-264]. Very recently, Lei et. al. demonstrated the tuning of Ni-based MOF-74 at various reaction durations and temperatures to modulate the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and storage [265]. The materials synthesized at $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 24h possessed the highest BET surface area $(1129\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{\Omega}$ ) and micropore volume $(0.46\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ) and thereby manifested superior performances. Moreover, post-synthesis modification can be another feasible route to improve the gas storage efficiency of MOFs. A recent article introduced such a method to improve the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas uptake capacity of MFU-4l MOF through post-synthesis metalation with Li et. al [266]. On the other hand, Srinivas et. al. illustrated that the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas storage capacity of GOFs could be tuned through the length of the boronic acid ligand [18]. The GOFs synthesized at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with 14PDBA ligand exhibited higher BET surface area $(470~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ with respect to that of GOF-1PBA $(442\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ) due to larger interlayer spacing for manifesting better $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas uptake capacity.
## 6. Conclusions and Remarks
The engineering techniques to tune the chemical structure, properties, and applications of three types of organic framework materials, i.e., MOFs, GOFs, and COFs were comprehensively reviewed. For MOFs, the variation of ligands might lead to the tuning of morphology, flexibility, porosity, and surface-active area. Moreover, MOFs yielded from reactions with functionalized ligands induced additional functionalities and improved the active surface area, while the adjustment of ligand length resulted in the manipulation of the pore size and volume. Furthermore, the reaction conditions such as temperature, solvent, and composition played a significant role in tuning the reaction pathways, crystal structure, crystallinity, and production yield, whereas the post-synthesis modification effectively modulated the surface area of MOFs. In comparison, the choice of ligands for synthesizing GOFs was limited to boronic acid linkers, although the interlayer spacing and the surface-active area were strongly dependent on the linker length and synthesis temperature. In addition, GOFs synthesized with different linker ratios showed variations in pore size or pore spacing. Finally, for the synthesis of COFs, variation of building block was important in modifying the structure, pore-volume, and surface area. Besides, the crystallinity and the reversibility of the synthesis reaction could be controlled by tuning the solvent and synthesis temperature, respectively, while post-synthesis modification of these materials might result in high adsorption and catalytic efficiencies. However, apart from crystallinity, porosity, structure, and surface area, the tuning strategies through variation of type and length of ligands, reaction conditions, and post-synthesis modifications significantly influenced the electronic, mechanical, chemical, and thermal properties of three types of organic framework materials as well as their applications in various fields such as catalysis, desalination, gas sensing, and gas storage.
This review gives an in-depth insight, which, in practice, will also help in the design and synthesis of novel organic framework materials. A subtle amendment in the synthesis route can facilitate the planning of innovative organic frameworks with different topologies, while currently there is still much room for further exploration. Moreover, the thirst to solve many environmental as well as societal problems can be quenched through the improvement and designing of innovative organic framework materials. Our prospects for the field are as follows:
i. There is an increasing trend in compositing organic framework materials with various nanomaterials to enable novel functionalities while harnessing the superior surface area. For example, plasmonic MOF nanocomposites were recently realized by incorporating noble metals and degenerate semiconductors in conventional MOF frameworks, which exhibited fascinating sensing and catalytic properties [247, 284]. The introduction of functional nanomaterials into the organic framework also brings in extra-scientific questions, such as the identification of the chemistry between the linker and functional nanomaterials and their roles in understanding tuning strategies, as well as the modification of the size, shape and surface functional group of the nanomaterials towards the overall properties of the organic framework materials.
The extension of the GOF to other 2D materials families might be of strong interest. Engineering of the surface properties of 2D materials (e.g, oxygen groups in 2D metal oxides, sulfide vacancies in 2D metal sulfide [285-288] may accommodate selected linkers to form the 3D hierarchical structure. The identification of appropriate types of linkers and the exploration of the functionalization technique can add new engineering dimensions to the tuning of 2D organic frameworks (2D-OFs).
iii. Similar to those implemented in MOFs, the complementary functional design of both the pores and skeletons of COFs through post-synthesis modification and defect engineering in a broader range can be possible. In addition, it is worthy to be devote additional efforts to exploring the functionalization techniques of COFs, and tuning the preparation methods may bring COFs with extraordinary characteristics which, in the future can open a new pathway to explore the effectiveness of COFs to solve more challenging energy and environmental related issues.   
iv. The 2D planar morphology is favorable in many surface area-related applications (e.g. catalysts and sensors) and therefore there should be a focus on broadening research areas of 2D MOFs. Moreover, atomically thin 2D MOFs may be an emerging candidate for next-generation multifunctional electronics, in addition to those based on 2D inorganic counterparts.  Although several synthesis strategies have been developed [36, 37, 289-291], there are still many difficulties yet to overcome, e.g., poor conductivity, easy aggregation, and thermal stability of MOFs.
